<description>
Build a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.
- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.

Rule:
- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.
- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.
- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.

Key insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "port pixels s (start cap pixel; green pixels adjacent to blobs)",
  "dst": "extend rays d to activate blobs",
  "path": "cast a straight ray from the start port; when a blob is hit, recolor it and emit straight thick rays from its port side through the green pixels; newly hit blobs activate and emit (BFS) until no new hits",
  "transform": "activated blob -> red; ray path -> red"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
task_id: 5961cc34

spec:
  grid: 13-27 rows x 15-27 cols, bg=8 (azure)
  colors: bg=8, blue=1 (blob body), green=3 (port marker), yellow=4 (start cap), red=2 (start seg / activated / rays)
  start_port:
    red_seg: vertical 1-wide red segment, height 2-3, bottom row touches grid bottom
    yellow_cap: single yellow pixel directly above red segment top
    column: start_col must differ from all blob bounding-box columns so starting ray travels straight up in bg
    clearance: at least 3 bg rows between yellow and the nearest blob above [observed]
  blob:
    count: 1-4 per grid (total across all components)
    shape: |
      symmetric diamond/octagon (LR and UD symmetric when green is counted as
      blue); combined blue+green bbox is one of ~3x4, 4x4, 5x4, 5x6, 6x6, 8x8;
      corners are notched so the silhouette looks like:
         ..##..     .##.     .####.     ..####..
         .####.     ####     ######     .######.
         ######     ####     ######     ########
         .####.     .##.     .####.     ########
         ..##..              ..##..     ...etc (8x8 octagon)
    port: 1-2 adjacent green pixels replacing blue cells on exactly ONE edge
          side of the diamond, forming a bar orthogonal to the emission direction;
          bar length = ray thickness (1 or 2 pixels)
    port_direction: one of up/down/left/right (blob -> green side)
    activation_chain: 0-3 blobs lie on the cascade path (start ray then each
      activated blob's port ray); at most 1 distractor blob (off every ray);
      most grids have chain_len >= 1 (~80%); chain_len weights ~[1,3,3,3]
  rays (in output only):
    start_ray: 1-wide red column from grid bottom up to first blob hit (or top edge)
    port_ray: thick red ray (width = port bar length) emitted from each activated
              blob along its port direction, extending until it hits another blob
              or leaves the grid
    cascade: BFS; every hit blob activates (turns red) and re-emits from its port
  output:
    activated blobs recolored to red; start segment kept red; yellow cap replaced by red;
    all traversed ray cells colored red; unhit blobs and greens are erased to bg

generation_strategy:
  approach: path-first — construct the cascade chain, then place distractors and
    invert to get the input
  steps:
    - pick grid size (H=13-27, W=15-27) and bg=8
    - pick chain_len in 0-3 (number of blobs the start ray activates in sequence);
      chain_len=0 means the start ray just exits the top
    - pick start_col and start_seg height (2-3), place yellow cap above it
    - simulate the cascade in the OUTPUT grid:
      * draw start ray upward from yellow cap as a 1-wide red line
      * for i in 1..chain_len: pick a blob size/shape/orientation such that the
        current ray hits it; place the blob so the incoming ray lands inside it;
        choose the next port_direction (differ from incoming direction, else
        blob would be a terminal); the new ray continues as thick red line (width
        = chosen port bar length 1 or 2) from the blob's port side
      * the final blob's outgoing ray extends to the grid boundary
    - add 0-1 distractor blobs placed in free cells NOT intersecting any ray
      path; each distractor has its own green port pointing to some direction
      but its ray is never drawn (still must not overlap other objects)
    - derive INPUT from output:
      * every activated blob: repaint red pixels inside its diamond footprint
        back to blue=1, and add its green port pixels on the chosen port side
      * erase all ray red pixels (turn back to bg)
      * keep start red segment and yellow cap as in the original placement
      * distractor blobs are drawn as blue+green in both input and output (but
        in output they remain unchanged / absent of red)
    - validate:
      * each blob's combined blue+green region is LR+UD symmetric
      * start_col does not fall inside any blob bbox column range (so the
        vertical start ray reaches the first intended blob)
      * each activated blob's port direction yields a straight clear ray to
        the next blob (or grid edge) with no other blob in between
      * distractors lie entirely off every ray line; >=1 bg cell separation
        from other blobs
      * solver output on the generated input equals the constructed output
```
